// channel.zm -- `Channel<T>` and the `select2` machinery. A channel is how two threads share
// anything at all; see docs/DESIGN.md's "Closures" and the actor entry.
//
// Part of Ziman's prelude: every file in lib/prelude/ is merged bare into every program by the
// loader, so everything here is callable with no `use` and `use prelude` does not resolve. In this
// directory `pub` does not control reachability -- a plain `fn` is just as callable -- it controls
// whether `ziman --api builtins` advertises the name, and `internal` is the one modifier that
// subtracts, taking a name out of `--api builtins` and out of editor completion while leaving it
// callable. Native functions are registered by prelude_register() in src/prelude.c, and each file's
// header is generated from its source by the Makefile's prelude rule.
//
// **A declaration lives in the file for the type its first argument has.** So `concat`'s string
// form is in string.zm and its array forms in array.zm, and `get_or`'s `Option` form is in
// option.zm with its two map forms in map.zm -- one overload set, read where each member's type
// lives.
//
// **`ziman --api builtins` prints an overload set as one block**, so the listing answers "what
// does this name accept" in one place even when the sources do not.
//
// That sentence was here, in all ten of these headers, as the reason splitting a set across files
// was safe -- and **it was false for a day**. The listing is built in file order, which grouped
// every set only while no set was ever split; checked on 2026-10-22, `filter` came out in two
// blocks 56 lines apart, `concat` in two, `get_or` in two, and `snapshot` and `clear` in **four**
// each. The listing now groups by name (a set appears where its first advertised member does, so
// the file order's topical reading survives), which made the claim true rather than deleting it.
// A pure reordering: 307 lines before and after, identical as sets.
//
// `docs/lib/prelude.md` is **not** grouped -- it is organised as one `##` section per file, so a
// split set still has a `###` heading in each. The listing is the one place to read a whole set.
//
// That reverses the older rule, which kept a split set whole in collections.zm. What remains in
// collections.zm is the names whose members span so many types that no one file is theirs -- `len`
// over nine, `contains` over nine, `remove` over six -- plus `map` and `flat_map`, which `map`'s
// Map and Set members pin there, `mutable`, whose fourth member takes a bare `T` and so has no
// file at all, and `filter`, down to four since its array pair moved to array.zm on 2026-10-22. **Everything else splits, however few files that
// means**: `snapshot` and `clear` across four each, `set` and `remove_all` across three, `get_or`
// and `update` across two. See docs/DESIGN.md's "Overloading available to a declaration".

// Which of `select2`'s two channels had a value first, and the value: `First(v)` from the
// `first` channel or `Second(v)` from the `second`. The parameters are named for the variants,
// so a call says which answer it is asking about: `select2(first: inbox, second: control)`.
// Named for what it is rather than a general `Either`, which programs commonly declare themselves
// and a bare prelude `Either` would then collide with. Available bare, like `Option`.
enum Selected<A, B> {
    First(A)
    Second(B)
}

//
// Declared, not defined -- the rule for each is a type-checker arm, as with every `builtin fn`.
// `Channel<T>` itself is a built-in type rather than a prelude struct, which is why none of these
// could be a `native fn`: a native's type is checked by the ordinary call path, and these are
// checked by the arm that knows what a channel is.

// Takes its element type from the context, like `ok`/`err`/`Some`: a `val` with a declared
// `Channel<T>`, a struct field, a `with` override, a returned value, or a parameter's type.
builtin fn channel_new<T>() -> Channel<T>

// Blocks until there is room; refused on a closed channel.
builtin fn send<T>(channel: Channel<T>, value: T)

// False when the channel is full or closed, rather than blocking.
builtin fn try_send<T>(channel: Channel<T>, value: T) -> bool

// Blocks until a value arrives. A closed and drained channel is a runtime error here --
// `receive_option` is the shape that answers "closed" as a value.
builtin fn receive<T>(channel: Channel<T>) -> T

// None when the channel is empty, rather than blocking.
builtin fn try_receive<T>(channel: Channel<T>) -> Option<T>

// None when the channel is closed and drained.
builtin fn receive_option<T>(channel: Channel<T>) -> Option<T>

// None when `millis` elapses first.
builtin fn receive_timeout<T>(channel: Channel<T>, millis: int) -> Option<T>

builtin fn channel_len<T>(channel: Channel<T>) -> int

builtin fn channel_close<T>(channel: Channel<T>)

// Whichever of the two has a value first, and which one it was. `first` and `second` are the
// `Selected` variants' names, not an order of preference -- neither channel is favoured.
builtin fn select2<A, B>(first: Channel<A>, second: Channel<B>) -> Selected<A, B>

// A guard sends `message` on `channel` when nothing references it any more: a database
// connection holds one whose message is its "close" request, so a connection dropped without an
// explicit close still ends the thread that owns its socket (once the garbage collector notices).
struct Guard {
    handle: int
}

// A guard that sends `message` on `channel` when it is collected.
native fn guard_new<T>(channel: Channel<T>, message: T) -> Guard
